‎Lesson 1: What Is Marketing?
‎
‎Marketing is the process of identifying customer needs, creating products or services that meet those needs, communicating their value, and building lasting relationships that benefit both the customer and the business.
‎
‎Marketing is not just advertising or convincing people to buy something. It begins long before a product is sold and continues long after the sale through customer support, follow-up, and relationship building.
‎
‎Simple Definition
‎
‎Marketing is helping the right people discover the right solution to their problems.
‎
‎---
‎
‎Lesson 2: Why Marketing Matters
‎
‎Without marketing, even the best product may never reach the people who need it.
‎
‎Marketing helps businesses:
‎
‎- Create awareness.
‎- Attract customers.
‎- Increase sales.
‎- Build trust.
‎- Retain customers.
‎- Grow sustainably.
‎- Understand customer needs.
‎- Stay competitive.
‎
‎Example
‎
‎A company develops excellent accounting software but tells no one about it. Another company develops similar software and actively educates businesses through demonstrations, articles, and social media. The second company is more likely to gain customers because effective marketing connects solutions with people who need them.
‎
‎---
‎
‎Lesson 3: Marketing vs. Selling
‎
‎Many people think marketing and selling are the same, but they are different.
‎
‎Marketing
‎
‎Marketing focuses on understanding customer needs, creating value, building trust, and attracting interested customers.
‎
‎Selling
‎
‎Selling focuses on completing a purchase after a customer has shown interest.
‎
‎Marketing builds relationships. Selling completes transactions.
‎
‎---
‎
‎Lesson 4: Understanding Customer Needs and Wants
‎
‎Every successful marketer understands customers before promoting products.
‎
‎Need
‎
‎A need is something essential for daily life or solving an important problem.
‎
‎Examples:
‎
‎- Food
‎- Water
‎- Shelter
‎- Education
‎- Security
‎
‎Want
‎
‎A want is the specific way a person chooses to satisfy a need.
‎
‎Example:
‎
‎Need:
‎Transportation
‎
‎Want:
‎A motorcycle, a bicycle, or a car.
‎
‎Great marketers discover both needs and wants before recommending solutions.
‎
‎---
‎
‎Lesson 5: The Marketing Process
‎
‎A professional marketer follows these steps:
‎
‎1. Identify customer problems.
‎2. Study the target audience.
‎3. Develop a suitable solution.
‎4. Set an appropriate price.
‎5. Choose the best distribution channels.
‎6. Promote the product honestly.
‎7. Build customer relationships.
‎8. Gather feedback.
‎9. Improve the product continuously.
‎
‎Marketing is an ongoing process, not a one-time event.
‎
‎---
‎
‎Lesson 6: The 4 Ps of Marketing
‎
‎The four core elements of marketing are:
‎
‎Product
‎
‎What is being offered to customers.
‎
‎Price
‎
‎The amount customers pay and the value they receive.
‎
‎Place
‎
‎Where and how customers can obtain the product or service.
‎
‎Promotion
‎
‎The methods used to inform and persuade customers, such as social media, email, events, referrals, or demonstrations.
‎
‎---
‎
‎Lesson 7: Types of Marketing
‎
‎Traditional Marketing
‎
‎Examples:
‎
‎- Newspapers
‎- Radio
‎- Television
‎- Billboards
‎- Flyers
‎
‎Digital Marketing
‎
‎Marketing through online channels.
‎
‎Examples:
‎
‎- Websites
‎- Email
‎- Search engines
‎- Online advertisements
‎
‎Social Media Marketing
‎
‎Using platforms such as Facebook, Instagram, LinkedIn, TikTok, YouTube, WhatsApp, Telegram, and X to build awareness and engage customers.
‎
‎Organic (Relationship) Marketing
‎
‎This is an important approach at Zentrix.
‎
‎Organic marketing means using genuine relationships and trust to introduce products to people who may benefit from them.
‎
‎Examples include:
‎
‎- Friends
‎- Family
‎- Colleagues
‎- Classmates
‎- Neighbours
‎- Existing customers
‎- Professional contacts
‎
‎Organic marketing should always be ethical. The goal is to help people, not pressure them.
‎
‎Referral Marketing
‎
‎Encouraging satisfied customers to recommend products to others.
‎
‎Affiliate Marketing
‎
‎Independent marketers earn commissions by referring customers who complete purchases.
‎
‎---
‎
‎Lesson 8: Products and Services
‎
‎Physical Products
‎
‎Items customers can touch.
‎
‎Examples:
‎
‎- Clothing
‎- Furniture
‎- Phones
‎- Computers
‎
‎Digital Products
‎
‎Products delivered electronically.
‎
‎Examples:
‎
‎- Software
‎- Mobile apps
‎- E-books
‎- Online courses
‎- Templates
‎
‎Services
‎
‎Activities performed for customers.
‎
‎Examples:
‎
‎- Web development
‎- Graphic design
‎- Software consulting
‎- Business consulting
‎- Training
‎
‎---
‎
‎Lesson 9: Business Models
‎
‎B2B (Business-to-Business)
‎
‎One business sells to another business.
‎
‎Example:
‎
‎Zentrix develops payroll software for companies.
‎
‎B2C (Business-to-Consumer)
‎
‎Businesses sell directly to individual customers.
‎
‎Example:
‎
‎Selling a productivity app through an app store.
‎
‎C2C (Consumer-to-Consumer)
‎
‎Individuals sell products or services to other individuals through marketplaces.
‎
‎---
‎
